{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0115","statement":"The paper presents methods for learning multi-level representations of entities on three complementary levels: character, word, and entity.","source_span":"three complementary levels: character (character patterns in entity names extracted, e.g., by neural networks), word (embeddings of words in entity names) and entity (entity embeddings)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0115","statement":"The subword model of fasttext (Bojanowski et al., 2016) performs best on the character level.","source_span":"the subword model of fasttext (Bojanowski et al., 2016) on the character level"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0115","statement":"Word2vec (Mikolov et al., 2013) performs best on the word level.","source_span":"for word2vec (Mikolov et al., 2013) on the word level"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0115","statement":"The order-aware model wang2vec (Ling et al., 2015a) performs best on the entity level.","source_span":"for the order-aware model wang2vec (Ling et al., 2015a) on the entity level"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0115","statement":"A joint representation of all three levels (character, word, entity) improves the existing embedding-based baseline for fine-grained entity typing by a large margin.","source_span":"a joint representation of all three levels improves the existing embedding based baseline for fine-grained entity typing by a large margin"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0115","statement":"Adding information from entity descriptions further improves multi-level representations of entities.","source_span":"adding information from entity descriptions further improves multi-level representations of entities"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0115","statement":"Examples of knowledge bases mentioned are Freebase, Wikipedia, Google knowledge graph, and YAGO.","source_span":"Examples of such KBs are Freebase BIBREF3 , Wikipedia, Google knowledge graph and YAGO BIBREF4"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0115","statement":"The authors released their dataset and source code at cistern.cis.lmu.de/figment2/.","source_span":"We release our dataset and source codes: cistern.cis.lmu.de/figment2/."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0115","statement":"A three-token sequence containing an initial like 'P.' surrounded by two capitalized words, e.g., 'Rolph P. Kugl', is likely to refer to a person.","source_span":"a three-token sequence containing an initial like “P.” surrounded by two capitalized words (“Rolph P. Kugl”) is likely to refer to a person"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0115","statement":"The word-level representation is computed as the sum of the embeddings of the words that make up the entity name.","source_span":"We compute the word-level representation as the sum of the embeddings of the words that make up the entity name."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0115","fact_refs":[1,2,3],"question":"Which embedding methods perform best at the character, word, and entity levels of entity representation, according to this paper?","answer":"Fasttext's subword model (Bojanowski et al., 2016) works best at the character level, word2vec (Mikolov et al., 2013) at the word level, and the order-aware model wang2vec (Ling et al., 2015a) at the entity level."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0115","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What effect does combining all three levels of entity representation have on fine-grained entity typing performance?","answer":"It improves the existing embedding-based baseline for fine-grained entity typing by a large margin."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0115","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What additional source of information further improves the multi-level entity representations described in this paper?","answer":"Adding information from entity descriptions further improves the multi-level representations of entities."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0115","fact_refs":[6],"question":"What knowledge bases does the paper give as examples of structured stores of entity knowledge?","answer":"Freebase, Wikipedia, Google knowledge graph, and YAGO."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0115","fact_refs":[7],"question":"Where did the authors release the dataset and source code for this entity typing work?","answer":"At cistern.cis.lmu.de/figment2/."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0115","fact_refs":[8],"question":"What example does the paper give of a character-level pattern that signals a name likely refers to a person?","answer":"A three-token sequence with an initial like 'P.' surrounded by two capitalized words, e.g., 'Rolph P. Kugl', is likely to refer to a person."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0116","statement":"NER research on recognising and classifying named entities dates back to the first MUC challenge in 1995.","source_span":"starting from the first MUC challenge in 1995"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0116","statement":"An example of an unusual surface form for a named entity is 'Szeged' appearing as an unusual character sequence in an English-language document.","source_span":"unusual character sequences for the given language (e.g. Szeged in an English-language document)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0116","statement":"'The White House' is given as an example of words that individually are typically not named entities but become one when combined.","source_span":"words that individually are typically not NEs, unless they are combined together (e.g. the White House)"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0116","statement":"The study includes seven benchmark NER corpora, spanning newswire, broadcast conversation, Web content, and social media.","source_span":"seven benchmark NER corpora are included, spanning newswire, broadcast conversation, Web content, and social media"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0116","statement":"The corpora studied are OntoNotes, ACE, MUC 7, the Ritter NER corpus, the MSM 2013 corpus, and the UMBC Twitter corpus.","source_span":"the corpora studied are OntoNotes BIBREF29 , ACE BIBREF30 , MUC 7 BIBREF31 , the Ritter NER corpus BIBREF21 , the MSM 2013 corpus BIBREF32 , and the UMBC Twitter corpus BIBREF33"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0116","statement":"To eliminate bias from the choice of statistical NER approach, experiments were carried out with three differently-principled NER approaches: Stanford NER, SENNA, and CRFSuite.","source_span":"experiments are carried out with three differently-principled NER approaches, namely Stanford NER BIBREF34 , SENNA BIBREF35 and CRFSuite BIBREF36"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0116","statement":"Corpora including only domain-specific entities, such as biomedical corpora, were excluded from the study.","source_span":"corpora including only domain-specific entities (e.g. biomedical corpora) were excluded"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0116","fact_refs":[0],"question":"In what year did the first MUC challenge take place, marking an early milestone in NER research?","answer":"1995."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0116","fact_refs":[1,2],"question":"What examples does the paper give of named entities with unusual surface forms that make NER recall difficult?","answer":"'Szeged' as an unusual character sequence in an English-language document, and 'the White House', where individually common words form a named entity only when combined."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0116","fact_refs":[3],"question":"How many benchmark NER corpora does this study use, and what text genres do they span?","answer":"Seven benchmark corpora, spanning newswire, broadcast conversation, Web content, and social media."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0116","fact_refs":[4],"question":"Which six specific corpora are used in this quantitative analysis of NER generalisation?","answer":"OntoNotes, ACE, MUC 7, the Ritter NER corpus, the MSM 2013 corpus, and the UMBC Twitter corpus."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0116","fact_refs":[5],"question":"Which three NER systems were used in the experiments to avoid bias from a single approach?","answer":"Stanford NER, SENNA, and CRFSuite."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0116","fact_refs":[6],"question":"Why were corpora such as biomedical datasets excluded from this NER generalisation study?","answer":"Because they include only domain-specific entities, whereas the study focuses on corpora annotated with the same or similar entity classes (people, locations, organisations)."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0117","statement":"AntSynNET is a novel neural network model that exploits lexico-syntactic patterns from syntactic parse trees to distinguish antonyms from synonyms.","source_span":"we present a novel neural network model AntSynNET that exploits lexico-syntactic patterns from syntactic parse trees"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0117","statement":"AntSynNET integrates the distance between the related words along the syntactic path as a new pattern feature.","source_span":"we successfully integrate the distance between the related words along the syntactic path as a new pattern feature"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0117","statement":"All pattern features are fed into a recurrent neural network with long short-term memory (LSTM) units to encode patterns as vector representations.","source_span":"All pattern features are fed into a recurrent neural network with long short-term memory (LSTM) units BIBREF8 , which encode the patterns as vector representations"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0117","statement":"Justeson and Katz (1991) suggested that adjectival opposites co-occur with each other in specific linear sequences, such as 'between X and Y'.","source_span":"Justeson/Katz:91 suggested that adjectival opposites co-occur with each other in specific linear sequences, such as between X and Y"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0117","statement":"Hearst (1992) determined surface patterns, e.g., 'X such as Y', to identify nominal hypernyms.","source_span":"Hearst:92 determined surface patterns, e.g., X such as Y, to identify nominal hypernyms"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0117","statement":"Lin (2003) proposed two textual patterns, 'from X to Y' and 'either X or Y', indicating semantic incompatibility to distinguish opposites from semantically similar words.","source_span":"Lin2003 proposed two textual patterns indicating semantic incompatibility, from X to Y and either X or Y, to distinguish opposites from semantically similar words"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0117","statement":"Results from classification experiments show that AntSynNET improves performance over prior pattern-based methods.","source_span":"The results from classification experiments show that AntSynNET improves the performance over prior pattern-based methods."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d2_doc0117","statement":"The implementation of the AntSynNET models is made publicly available.","source_span":"the implementation of our models is made publicly available"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0117","fact_refs":[0,1],"question":"What new feature does the AntSynNET model add on top of standard lexico-syntactic pattern information for distinguishing antonyms and synonyms?","answer":"It integrates the distance between the two related words along the syntactic path in the parse tree, in addition to the lexical and syntactic pattern information."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0117","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What type of neural network does AntSynNET use to encode lexico-syntactic patterns as vector representations?","answer":"A recurrent neural network with long short-term memory (LSTM) units."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0117","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What linear sequence pattern did Justeson and Katz (1991) suggest that adjectival opposites co-occur in?","answer":"Specific linear sequences such as 'between X and Y'."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0117","fact_refs":[4],"question":"What surface pattern did Hearst (1992) use to identify nominal hypernyms?","answer":"Patterns like 'X such as Y'."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0117","fact_refs":[5],"question":"What two textual patterns did Lin (2003) propose to distinguish opposites from semantically similar word pairs?","answer":"'from X to Y' and 'either X or Y', both indicating semantic incompatibility."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d2_doc0117","fact_refs":[6],"question":"How did AntSynNET's performance compare to prior pattern-based methods for distinguishing antonyms and synonyms?","answer":"AntSynNET improved the classification performance over prior pattern-based methods."}
